# Line endings are decided here rather than by whichever machine committed
# last. Ubuntu is the deployment target and LF is what it gets.
#
# Without this the entry-level Windows rig quietly commits CRLF the first time
# anyone regenerates a committed corpus: the generators in nmos/**/tests/
# (_messages_corpus.py, _wire_corpus.py, _persist_corpus.py, the codegen ones)
# and nmos/cluster/tests/_layout_corpus.py all emit through Path.write_text,
# which translates "\n" on that platform. A one-value change then arrives as a
# whole-file diff, and the review that was supposed to catch the one value
# cannot see it. `core.autocrlf` is per-machine and was not going to be set on
# everyone's; this is.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except what Windows itself parses. cmd.exe mishandles LF-only batch files --
# labels and goto in particular read past the end of the intended line -- so
# these are checked out with CRLF on every platform.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# And what Linux executes, which must never acquire a CR: a shebang ending in
# "\r" makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage
# return, and the failure reads "No such file or directory" about a path that
# is plainly there.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Byte-identical copies of files maintained in the Matrox SDP project, which
# are CRLF at the source. Held that way by discipline, as the fake-as modules
# are -- normalising them here would make every future comparison against
# upstream a whole-file diff, which is the exact problem this file is for.
sdp/MatroxSdp.py -text
sdp/MatroxSdpCheck.py -text
sdp/MatroxSdpWrite.py -text

# Never transformed, never diffed as text.
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
